  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "bit_read_BB",
    "file": "libredwg/src/bits.c",
    "description": "`bit_read_BB` uses `CHK_OVERFLOW`, whose test permits the end cursor (`dat->byte == dat->size`, `dat->bit == 0`) because it checks only `>` rather than whether the requested two bits are available. It then unconditionally reads `dat->chain[dat->byte]` at line 198, causing a one-byte heap-buffer-overflow. The object-bound setup in `dwg_decode_add_object` is valid: it restricts `dat` to the declared object size, which may legitimately end exactly at the allocation boundary. The bit reader must reject a read when no current byte exists, ideally by checking that two bits remain with overflow-safe arithmetic.",
    "conditions": "A malformed AC1014/R14 DWG is supplied through `LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput`, passes section/object-map parsing, and describes an in-bounds object whose declared data ends exactly at the input allocation boundary. The object type decodes as ATTDEF. Earlier ATTDEF fields consume all declared object bytes, so `FIELD_BS0(generation)` in `dwg.spec:470` calls `bit_read_BS` and then `bit_read_BB` with `dat->byte == dat->size` and `dat->bit == 0`. The faulty boundary check accepts this state and reads one byte beyond the restricted object/input buffer."
  },
